---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import BranchRivers from "../components/BranchRivers.astro";
---
<Base title="The Lines"
      eyebrow="Where this family went, and who went with them"
      dek="Seven generations never left Arienzo. Then, inside a single lifetime, the line moved four times and crossed the world. This is that movement — and, beneath it, the other Falco of the same parish and the same street whom no record joins to it.">

  <BranchRivers />

  <h2>How to read the diagram</h2>
  <p><strong>The spine is places, not names.</strong> Names hang beneath the place they belong to.
  A family drawn <em>above</em> the channel married into the line <strong>and brought a new town with
  it</strong> — and there are only three: the <a href={u("/morgillo")}>Morgillo</a> of San Felice a
  Cancello at generation four, the <a href={u("/annecchino")}>Annecchino</a> of Forchia at generation
  seven, and the <a href={u("/zampiello")}>Zampiello</a> of Arpaia at generation eight. The families
  marked in ochre <em>on</em> the channel — Crisci, Rivetti, Arricole, Maione — married in without
  moving anyone, because the bride was already of Arienzo.</p>
  <p><strong>Forchia appears twice and is the same town both times.</strong> That is the causal
  hinge of the whole geography: Carminantonio Falco married Filomena Annecchino, the Annecchino were
  of Forchia, and so his son was born there in 1930. Seven generations had not moved; a marriage
  moved them.</p>
  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>The Zampiello are not a parallel family and are no longer drawn as one.</strong> An
    earlier version of this diagram ran them alongside the line as a separate stream. Francesca
    Zampiello married Carmine Falco at Benevento in 1955 and sailed with him in 1964 — <strong>from
    generation eight onward they are this family</strong>. The same version omitted San Felice a
    Cancello and Forchia altogether, and used one visual grammar for places, for people and once for
    a ship. <a href={u("/corrections")}>Corrections →</a></p>
  </div>

  <h2>The rule this page follows</h2>
  <p>A shared surname in one small town is <strong>suggestive and not evidence</strong>. Two Falco
  households on the same street may be brothers, cousins, or unrelated men who happen to share a
  common Campanian name. This archive joins two people only when a record says so, and draws
  everything else apart — even where joining them would make a better story.</p>

  <h2>What changed in May 1831</h2>
  <p>Until that register was read, the honest position was that <em>none</em> of the strada Camellara
  households could be joined to the line. That is no longer quite true.</p>
  <blockquote>
    …sono comparsi <P n="Andrea Crisci" bold={true} />, di anni cinquantotto, contadino, strada Camellara,
    <em>vicino della defunta</em>, e <P n="Vincenzo Falco" bold={true} />, di anni trentotto, contadino,
    strada Camellara, <strong><em>parente della defunta</em></strong>…
    <span class="small">— Arienzo, Morti 1831, atto 33, on the death of Anna Falco, thirteen months,
    daughter of Michelangelo Falco and Mariantonia Vigliotta</span>
  </blockquote>
  <p>The clerk drew the distinction himself: one declarant a <em>neighbour</em>, the other a
  <strong>kinsman</strong>. Vincenzo Falco, thirty-eight in 1831, is generation three of this line.
  So <strong>Michelangelo Falco's household is kin</strong> — degree unstated. The other three
  Camellara households remain unjoined. One joined, three not.
  <a href={u("/wider-family")}>The wider family →</a></p>
</Base>
